<figure id="A7.F13" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:198.7pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01171v1/rng_distribution_comparison.svg" id="A7.SS5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/123;" width="274" height="123"></object>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Dice roll distributions from direct, sequence, and verbalized sampling prompting with Gemini-2.5-Pro. The red dashed line marks the expected uniform distribution: VS aligns most closely, sequence follows, while direct prompting collapses to a single mode (e.g., 4).

</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="A7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also study if Verbalized Sampling (VS) can perform the task of random number generation, which is important for tasks that require unpredictability in random processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, e.g., paper-scissor-stone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">West &amp; Potts, 2025</a>)</cite>.
To evaluate this, we assess whether VS enables LLMs to better approximate random behavior in a simple setting: rolling a fair 6-sided dice. For each method, we prompt the model to simulate a dice roll, sampling <math id="A7.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=600</annotation></semantics></math> responses and <math id="A7.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math> responses for each LLM call. We then calculate the KL divergence between the empirical distribution of the generated numbers and the true uniform distribution. This allows us to quantitatively assess how well each method captures true randomness.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A7.T26" title="In G.5 Random Number Generation ‣ Appendix G Detailed Experimental Results ‣ Verbalized Sampling: How to Mitigate Mode Collapse and Unlock LLM Diversity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a> presents the average KL divergence across models for the dice roll experiment using different prompting methods. Figure <a href="#A7.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ G.5 Random Number Generation ‣ Appendix G Detailed Experimental Results ‣ Verbalized Sampling: How to Mitigate Mode Collapse and Unlock LLM Diversity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> offers a detailed visualization of the dice roll distributions under direct, sequence, and VS prompting with Gemini-2.5-Pro.
Direct prompting produces a highly skewed distribution, often collapsing to a single outcome (e.g., rolling a 4), which is reflected in a high KL divergence (<math id="A7.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.926" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.926</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.926</annotation></semantics></math>). Direct with chain-of-thought performs even worse (<math id="A7.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.163" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.163</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.163</annotation></semantics></math>), while multi-turn improves but remains skewed (<math id="A7.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.119" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.119</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.119</annotation></semantics></math>). In contrast, both sequence prompting (<math id="A7.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.058" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.058</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.058</annotation></semantics></math>) and our VS variants achieve distributions that closely approximate the expected uniform distribution. Among them, VS-Standard achieves the lowest KL divergence, followed closely by VS-Multi and VS-CoT. These results confirm that VS improves LLM performance on random number generation over baselines, and aligns more closely with the expected uniform distribution.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.6 </span>Synthetic Data Generation</h3>

<section id="A7.SS6.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">G.6.1 </span>Positive Synthetic Data Generation</h4>

<div id="A7.SS6.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS6.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show more detail on the positive synthetic data generation task.</p>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS6.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Synthetic Data Generation Setup.</h5>

<div id="A7.SS6.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS6.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure comparable results with related work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we use the same temperature of <math id="A7.SS6.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6</annotation></semantics></math> and top-p of <math id="A7.SS6.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> for the answer generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS6.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finetuning on Synthetic Data.</h5>

<div id="A7.SS6.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS6.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The training is done with 5 epochs and a learning rate of <math id="A7.SS6.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5e-6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5e-6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T27" class="ltx_table">
